\section{Experiments}
\label{sec:results}

\subsection{Setup}
\label{sec:setup}

\paragraph{Models.} gpt-oss-20b and gpt-oss-120b \citep{openai2025gptoss}, with their released MXFP4
weights served by vLLM \citep{kwon2023vllm}, and Gemma-4-26B-A4B-it \citep{google2026gemma4}, a
mixture-of-experts model, standard checkpoint, queried through OpenRouter and pinned to a single provider on
MATH and served with vLLM at bf16 on the other three benchmarks. The cheap rung $L_0$
uses each model's cheapest reasoning setting (low effort for gpt-oss, thinking off for Gemma) and greedy
decoding; $L_1$ and every call of the pipeline use the default setting (medium effort, thinking on) and
sample at temperature 1.0, with top-$p$ 0.95 and top-$k$ 64 for Gemma as its model card recommends.
Token log-probabilities are returned for the calls that score $L_0$ and $L_1$. Each call has a
generation cap of 8{,}192 tokens at $L_0$ and of 4{,}096 (gpt-oss on MATH, FRAMES and MuSiQue) or
16{,}384 (all other runs) elsewhere; a reply that spends its cap without an answer receives one closing
call with reasoning off.

\paragraph{Benchmarks.} MATH-500 \citep{hendrycks2021math,lightman2024verify}, FRAMES
\citep{krishna2025frames}, answerable MuSiQue validation \citep{trivedi2022musique}, and BIG-Bench Extra Hard
(BBEH; \citealp{kazemi2025bbeh}). MATH, FRAMES and MuSiQue were split once into a development split (20\%),
used to build the pipeline, and a test split (80\%) used for all results: 400, 660 and 1{,}934 tasks. For
BBEH, which played no part in building the pipeline, we draw 38 problems from each of its 23 tasks with a
fixed seed, 874 in all, and use them all. FRAMES and
MuSiQue are open-book. Each MuSiQue question comes with its twenty paragraphs, supporting and distracting
(median 2.4k tokens). Each FRAMES question gets the Wikipedia articles it links to (two to nineteen,
median 17k tokens), converted from their source with infoboxes and tables kept as text; when a question's
articles exceed 80k tokens, each is cut to a share of that limit from its end (6 of 660 questions), and 3 of
the linked articles no longer exist. The documents precede the question in every call, so every rung and
every agent pays for them. MATH is scored by symbolic equivalence and BBEH by the fuzzy match of its official
evaluation; FRAMES and MuSiQue, where
exact match rejects correct but verbose answers, are scored by an LLM judge (gpt-oss-120b, validated
against exact match and by hand; Appendix~\ref{app:details}). Cost is prompt plus completion tokens,
including hidden reasoning, over all calls a task makes, tool calls included.

\paragraph{Records and replay.} For every task we record two runs: the reference pipeline, run from
scratch, and \climb{}'s ladder forced through every cheap rung, which yields the outcome, cost and
confidence of $L_0$ and $L_1$. Replaying these records evaluates any policy on any subset of tasks with
the costs and outcomes of the recorded outputs; an escalating task receives the reference's outcome
and pays its cost on top of the rungs already run. For 300 tasks per setting we also record a second,
independent run of the reference, to tell recurring harms from sampling variation.

\paragraph{Evaluation protocol.} The loss of every policy is measured against the reference.
Tolerances are absolute: $\eps = 10\%$ allows a loss of 10 accuracy points whatever the reference
accuracy. We draw 200 random calibration/evaluation splits (50/50), or 20/40/40 for policies that need
training data, and report means. The \emph{violation rate} is the share of splits in which the selected
policy's loss on the full recorded pool exceeds $\eps$, a diagnostic of the finite-pool replay;
\S\ref{sec:main} checks the event of Proposition~\ref{prop:risk} directly.

\paragraph{Baselines.} The reference pipeline; uncertified rules (always stopping at $L_0$ or at $L_1$,
and a fixed confidence threshold that spends as much as \climb{}); an oracle that stops at the cheapest
rung that answers correctly; and richer escalation policies certified exactly as \climb{} is
(\S\ref{sec:variants}).
